      ## Purpose
      
      Write the change's WHAT and WHY, its release note, its capability routing, and
      its open questions.
      
      ## Release note
      
      The proposal carries exactly one release note: one complete, user-facing
      sentence naming what the change does. Every revision the change records
      carries the release note as its message subject; the scenario title goes in
      the message body. A changelog line derived from revision subjects is a consequence, not the
      release note.
      
      ## WHAT
      
      State the observable behavior added, modified, or removed. Name the consumer
      and the outcome.
      
      ## WHY
      
      State the problem and the consequence of leaving it unsolved. Do not restate
      the solution.
      
      ## Capability routing
      
      Record each capability as `NEW in <group>`, `MODIFIED <capability>`, or
      `REMOVED <capability>`. A NEW capability names the group whose use case will
      own it.
      
      ## Open questions
      
      Record unresolved decisions with ids `Q1`, `Q2`, ... Each question states what
      it blocks and which artifact will resolve it. If none remain, say so explicitly.
      
      ## Change strategy
      
      Every change records exactly one change strategy in its design: `incremental
      modification`, `Constant Ise`, or a whole-product rebuild. A change that
      rebuilds also records its `Ise Test` mode beside the strategy.
      
